bove 200 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:  \nYou hold **Jack of Diamonds** and **3 of Clubs**. The board shows: **Ace of Spades, Queen of Clubs, 10 of Diamonds, 5 of Hearts**.  \nThis gives you four community cards — A, Q, 10, 5 — with your J and 3.\n\nLooking at possible hands:\n- You have **J♦**, and the board has **Q♣, 10♦, A♠** — that’s **A-Q-J-10**, missing only a **K** for a straight (A-high straight: 10-J-Q-K-A).\n- You are one card away from a **broadway straight** (10 through A). However, no King is present yet.\n- There is **no flush draw** for you: only one diamond (10♦, J♦), two clubs (Q♣), and one heart (5♥), so you don’t have four to a flush.\n- Your 3 of Clubs is likely worthless; it doesn't pair the board or contribute to any strong draw.\n- You currently have **top pair with Jack high**, but **Jack is not paired on the board**, and the board has A, Q, 10 — all higher than your Jack except the 5 and 3. So even top pair isn't strong here.\n\nThe opponent raised by 100 in Round 2 (the turn), which suggests strength — possibly they hit a strong hand like top pair (A-x, Q-x), two pair, or even a made straight if they held K-x.\n\nYour position:\n- You checked the flop (Round 1), then opponent called.\n- On the turn (Round 2), you checked again, and now **opponent raised 100 after your check**, indicating aggression and likely a strong hand.\n- You now face a decision: fold, call 100 more, raise, or go all-in.\n\nCurrent pot size: Let's estimate:\n- Pre-flop: both players likely posted blinds (assume SB = 100, BB = 200) → pot = 300\n- Flop: you bet 100, opponent called → +200 → pot ≈ 500\n- Turn: you checked, opponent raised 100 (so now total bet this round is 100), you must call 100 to continue.\n→ Current pot ≈ 600 before your action.\n\nCalling 100 to win ~600 gives you 6:1 odds — but you have very little equity unless you catch a King (8 outs for a straight: any K, 4 in deck) — but even then, if opponent