he bet), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:  \nYou hold **King of Hearts, 10 of Clubs**. The board shows: **3♦, 4♠, A♥, Q♥**.  \nThis gives you four community cards — and you’re on the **Turn** (since four public cards are out). The River is next after this betting round.\n\nYou currently have an **open-ended straight draw** (you need a **J** to complete Q-J-10-K-A straight) and a **flush draw** in hearts (you have K♥ and there are two hearts on the board: A♥ and Q♥; you need another heart on the river for a flush). However, with only one card to come, you're not there yet.\n\nPossible strong hands:\n- **Straight Draw**: You need a **Jack** (any suit) to make a King-high straight (A-K-Q-J-10). This gives you 4 outs (four Jacks in the deck).\n- **Flush Draw**: There are 9 hearts total needed for a flush. Two are in your hand (K♥), two on board (A♥, Q♥). So two hearts used — 5 already seen. That leaves 9 - 2 = 7 unseen hearts in the remaining 46 cards? Wait: total hearts in deck: 13. Board has A♥, Q♥. You have K♥. That’s three hearts accounted for. So 10 left in the deck? No — wait: total 13 hearts. Three are known (A♥, Q♥, K♥), so 10 remain unaccounted, but 9 other hearts (excluding the three used) could still be in play. But only 46 cards remain unseen. Number of outs for flush: 10 remaining hearts minus any that may be burned or in opponent's hand — conservatively, **9 outs** (standard flush draw).\n\nBut wait: do you have two hearts? Your hand: **K♥, 10♣** → only **one heart** in your hand. Board: A♥, Q♥ → two hearts. So total three hearts visible. Therefore, **10 hearts remain in the deck**, but only **9 outs** (since 13 total – 3 visible = 10 unseen, but one will come on river — so 10 possible, but only one card to come). So flush draw gives you **9 outs**.\n\nStraight draw: need a **Jack** → 4 outs.\n\nHowever, **J♥** would complete both straight and flush — so we must avoid double-counting. So total unique outs:  \n- Flush: 9 outs (remaining hearts)  \n- Straight: 4 outs (J♠, J♦, J♣, J♥)  \n- But J♥ is shared → so total outs = 9 + 4 - 1 = **12 outs**\n\nWith 12 outs and one card to come, your probability of hitting is roughly:  \n(12 / 46) ≈ **26% chance** to improve on the river.\n\nAdditionally, even if you don’t hit, your current best hand is **Ace-high with K kicker** — which is weak. If the opponent has any pair, they likely beat you.\n\nYour opponent has checked twice so far (on flop and turn), which may indicate weakness. But they called your pre-flop raise (you raised 200, they called), so they likely have a decent starting hand.\n\nGiven that, checking now is safe and allows you to see the river without investing more chips. Raising might get you folded out weaker hands, but it's unlikely — players who call to the turn usually have something. Bluff-raising here with a draw is risky and may backfire.\n\nAlternatively, a small raise (like 100) could be a semi-bluff, but given opponent’s passivity, they may just fold medium hands, and call only with better. Since you have position and the action is on you, checking maintains pot control.\n\nGoing all-in is too aggressive with only a draw and no prior aggression. Folding loses equity.\n\nBest move: **Check (0)** to see the river card.\n\nReason:  \nYou have a strong draw (12 outs) and good pot odds if you check and see the free card. Opponent has shown passivity by checking twice, possibly indicating a weak or marginal hand. Checking keeps the pot manageable and allows you to evaluate on the river. Raising risks building the pot when behind, and folding forfeits strong improvement chances.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
